exture s is defined as a sum of uh l2  norms between sp and ip  and  for all uh subs for all neighborhoods in  the output texture in practice we only  choose to work uh with a subset as uh  as star because it is a computationally  less expensive and  in general it is redundant to have the  all neighborhoods in the output texture  let's see how the algorithm performs so  we are given an input texture i and some  initialization of the output texture s  and we iterate the following two  procedures until convergence for each sp  we find the closest input neighborhood  ip and next we update the output texture  s  by optimizing the energy function that  was presented in the previous slide  as i mentioned we need some  initialization of the output texture as  to begin the synthesis procedure if we  are not provided with an image  initialization we randomly  assign input patches to ip and the last  question that remains since how when to  stop the optimization procedure and the  authors in this case suggest  hosting the optimization procedure when  ip stops changing  the optimization problem that was  presented in the previous slide can be  loosely treated as expectation  maximization  in the energy function we treat the  output texture as as variables while  eyepiece as parameters  then in the e-step we minimize the  texture energy function with respect to  s  and since the attached energy function  is quadratic we end up solving a system  of linear equations an interesting case  is when two patches in the output  texture s overlap namely sp and sq then  um  the in the overlap region the pixel  intensity values are drawn to the two  different input patches ip and iq and  therefore from this perspective the goal  of the e-step is to  assign the average of the corresponding  pixel intensity values from the input  texture to the  overlap region in the output texture in  the m step we minimize the texture  energy with respect to the  eyepiece  hence the parameters and this  corresponds to nearest neighbors search  that was also introducing the  image quilting algorithm and  authors implement nearest neighbors  search using kd trees  in addition to that the authors suggest  running the texture optimization  algorithm in a multi-resolution and  multi-level synthesis and the motivation  of this approach uh are the following  uh so the output uh texture  the result and the  convergence of the algorithm is  dependent on the initialization of the  output texture  in addition at the uh the patch size if  the if the patch size is small then  there is um  more variation in the output texture but  at the same time we may not capture  large scale structures and if the page  size is large then  large-scale verbatim copy zones may be  introduced in the output texture  and finally the  right source of the patch size is highly  dependent on the resolution  of the input texture  so the idea of applying multi-level and  multi-resolution synthesis is the  following we start at a very low  resolution and use different patch sizes  at each uh resolution from largest to  smallest large pet sizes help us capture  large gas structures while low uh small  pest sizes uh apply a further refinement  spread over the output texture  and at the next resolution level we use  the current resolutions output and  upsample it and use it as an  initialization to  uh to the next uh resolution level  uh this helps us avoid uh  getting stuck at that local optimum  now let's see the influence of  resolution levels on the output texture  as it can be noticed as we increase the  number of resolutions the visual quality  of the output texture increases and the  risks of garbage growing decrease as it  was the case when uh the number of  resolutions is equal to one  finally uh let's segue to uh neural  texture synthesis and begin our  discussion with a texture synthesis  using cnns by gades at all  this approach is a parametric texture  synthesis algorithm and therefore we  need to explicitly define a texture  model  the authors assume that textures are  stationary which means they they are  homogeneous and they don't contain large  scale structures  formally let's assume that we are given  an input texture i and our goal is to  synthesize an output texture i had the  texture model is computed  in the feature space of a pre-trained  vgg  the architecture of which is shown on  the right and the goal is to start from  a noise and  to start from a noise and optimize the  output texture to obtain a  uh output texture that has a similar  texture model as the given input texture  so in order to define  the texture model or the summary  statistic let's also assume that we are  given fl which is a matrix that contains  um  that contains the feature maps from  level l then formally the summary  statistics is a set of  gram matrices where each gram matrix is  an inner product between the feature  maps  from layer l  then in order to formulate the problem  as an optimization problem let's also  assume that we have we can we compute el  which is the  sum of square differences between the  gram matrices from level l between input  and output textures  and the loss function is formally then  defined uh as a weighted sum of els  now let's see how the whole algorithm  works we are given an input texture i  and some initialization of the output  texture i had  we begin by first computing the summary  statistics of the given input texture i  then we our goal is to optimize the loss  function that was presented in the  previous slide in order to obtain an  output texture i had  that has similar tests  similar summary statistic as the given  input texture let's also note at this  point that  the  gram matrices are calculated using only  convolutional or pulling layers and  therefore the output  can have arbitrary size  um in this case too we begin the out the  initialization  of the output texture  from a white noise and  the authors optimize the loss function  using lbfgs  let's also take a look at the influence  of layers that we use to calculate the  gram mattresses the name of the layer  that is shown on the slides  is the top most layers which means that  all the layer preceding layers have been  used in the calculation of the gram  mattresses  so it can be noticed that in the first  layers only the general structure of the  texture have been captured while if we  increase um  if we go deeper in the network then  um the visual quality of the output  texture improves and more details are  added to the output texture  the final  texture synthesis algorithm that we will  cover today is non-stationary texture  synthesis by joe at all  [Music]  this particular algorithm is designed to  handle non-stationary textures which  contain large-scale inhomogeneous  structures with spatial variation in  different attributes such as  color  local scale or orientation  in this approach we uh we are going to  use generative adversarial networks to  learn to generate output patches that  are two times larger than the given  input patch let's formally assume that  we are given an input example texture  and a patch from the given input texture  and uh  the goal of the generator network is to  generate an output texture that is two  times larger than the given input  texture so as uh in this case the output  texture is s of size two times w  and the goal of the discriminator  network is to classify whether the given  its input comes from the given input  example architecture or is generated by  the generator network  let's also note that we in this case we  deal with self-supervised learning  because uh the ground truth i is  directly taken from the  given input example texture the loss  function comprises of uh the adversarial  loss the l1 loss and the style loss  the other cellular loss in this case is  the main driving force while the l1 and  style losses are added to the total loss  in order to stabilize the training  reduce the artifacts and improve the  visual quality of the output texture  in this approach we need to train a  separate generator for each given input  example or texture  now let's take a look at the very  general arc architecture of the  generator network and the training  procedure  the generator network is fully  convoluted convolutional which implies  that it can take inputs of arbitrary  sizes uh particularly the given input  example texture can be given uh as an  input to the generator once it has been  trained in order to obtain a an output  texture that is two times larger than  the given input example texture  the generator network comprises of  convolutional layers and residual blocks  uh in six  in number so the residual blocks are  added to the architecture of the network  so we can go deeper in the network to  increase the receptive field of the  network and at the same time try to  avoid any potential problems we can have  during the training procedure the  receptive field at the end of the  last residual block is 106 which is very  close to the pad size w that the authors  use during the training procedure  the chaining works as follows uh we  randomly set at each iteration we  randomly select a a patch i of size 2  times w from the given input example  texture and  this i is going to serve as a ground  truth  uh next we randomly select the patch uh  w from the  from i and give it as an input to  the  to the generator the training procedure  is optimized using the adam optimizer  let's now see the influence of each loss  term on the output textures  so uh we begin with the adversarial loss  and we can notice that only the general  structure has been captured uh while as  we add the l1 and the style losses the  visual quality of the output texture  improves and  more color and details are added to the  output texture  let's also see the influence of the pad  size in this case  so the general idea here is that the  patch size should be large enough to  capture large-scale structures and  propagate it from the input texture to  the output texture  uh and this is especially essential when  we deal with non-stationary textures and  the passage should be small enough so as  we can  get  a distinct amount sufficient amount of  distinct  patches of size 2 times w to serve as  ground truth  let's now uh  go over a comparison among these four  algorithms  so the primary advantage of image  quilting texture optimization and  texture synthesis using cns is  controllability  in the case of image quilting  controllability refers to the idea that  we can modify the parameters so as to  handle most types of textures except two  non-stationary textures which cannot be  handled in three of the algorithms that  we saw today  in the case of texture optimization and  texture synthesis using cnns uh the  controllability refers to the idea that  we can modify the energy or loss  function so as to handle specific  texture cases or improve the visual  quality of the output texture  non-stationary texture synthesis can  handle all four types of textures  and and in addition to that uh  non-stationary texture synthesis can  generate very large textures if the  output texture is given a back is an  input to the generator  the speed is only an advantage in the  case of image quilting  uh while in the case of non-stationary  texture synthesis we have very fast  inference speed the training speed is uh  quite slow and another disadvantage of  non-stationary texture synthesis is that  it requires training a separate uh  generator network for each given input  example texture  let's go through some results and in  this case we can notice that the only in  the case of regular textures uh the only  algorithm that fails is nasty  is texture synthesis using cnns  in the case of irregular and stockhas  stochastic textures all four algorithms  provide satisfactory results for human  observation  and in the case of a non-stationary  textures we can notice that the only  only algorithm that has been able to  capture the non-stationarity and the  large-scale structure of the wood  is non-stationary texture synthesis  while the rest of the algorithms have  failed particularly  in the case of  texture synthesis using cnns only the  slot the style has been captured uh  while the large-scale inhomogeneous  structure of the wood hasn't been  captured by the algorithm  uh so let's also conclude  today's presentation so today i went  over uh four uh texture synthesis  algorithms uh classical and neural image  quilting is a fast and controllable  algorithm that handles regular irregular  and stochastic textures texture  optimization is a slow but controllable  algorithm and it handles all uh the same  types of textures as image quilting  uh texture synthesis using cnn's is a  slow but controllable algorithm and it  handles only irregular and stochastic  textures non-stationary texture  synthesis provides very fast inference  and it handles  all four types of textures that is a  regular irregular stochastic and  non-stationary textures  here are some references if any of you  is interested in further research  thank you for your attention  [Applause]
 

